clipnest (1.0.0) jammy; urgency=medium

  * fix(linux): password-manager copies are no longer saved to history.
    The marked copy itself was always refused, but GNOME re-publishes
    clipboard text without the marker once the source app lets go of the
    clipboard (KeePassXC's "clear after N seconds" does exactly that), and
    that re-published copy was being captured. Found on real hardware.
  * fix(linux): picking an item no longer freezes Clipnest ("not
    responding") when auto-paste isn't set up. Clipnest failed to recognise
    its own clipboard write, then asked itself for the clipboard contents
    from the very thread that has to answer, and waited 30 seconds. Its
    writes are now recognised exactly, with no timing involved.
  * fix(linux): the picker now closes when another window takes focus.
    It used to stay open behind that window, and the next hotkey press
    closed it instead of opening it.
  * fix(linux): choosing an item while a terminal is focused now pastes.
    Terminals are recognised through the accessibility bus and sent
    Ctrl+Shift+V. Before, they received Ctrl+V and printed "^V".
  * fix: the update check no longer offers to "update" to an older
    release when the installed build is newer than the latest release.
  * feat(linux): the tray menu gains "Pause Capture", matching the macOS
    menu bar. It shares one setting with Settings -> General -> "Enable
    clipboard capture".
  * fix: scripts/update.sh (macOS) applies the same newer-only rule, so it
    can no longer replace a newer build with an older release either.
  * Version 1.0.0: first release that ships the Linux port, verified on
    physical Ubuntu 24.04 hardware (GNOME 46, Wayland).

 -- Aayush Gour <ag14906@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

clipnest (0.9.4) jammy; urgency=medium

  * fix(linux): startup no longer blocks the GTK main thread. Connecting to
    IBus and reconciling a stale crash-safety marker from a previous
    force-quit both used to run inline on the main thread before the app
    window could appear; both now run in the background, so the app should
    no longer stall at launch — this was most noticeable right after a
    force-quit, since that is exactly when a crash-safety marker is left
    behind for the next launch to clean up. A small internal lock was added
    so the two reconciliation paths (normal startup and quit-time cleanup)
    can never run at the same time and step on each other's state.
  * fix(linux): Settings -> Permissions no longer shows the same status
    message twice (once in normal text, once again greyed out below it).
    The duplicate made a correctly-granted permission read as if something
    had gone wrong; the message is now shown once.
  * fix(linux): internal — per-call D-Bus timeouts are now actually
    enforced. The socket-level receive timeout was previously set once when
    a connection was opened and never updated for later calls, so a call
    asking for a short timeout could still block for as long as the
    connection's original, larger one. Measured: a call requesting a 100 ms
    timeout previously took up to 3017 ms; it now completes in 108 ms. This
    has no visible effect on its own.
  * Note: this release does not fix the reported freeze when picking an
    item from the picker. That issue remains unreproduced and under
    investigation; none of the fixes above touch the code path item
    selection uses.

 -- Aayush Gour <ag14906@gmail.com>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

clipnest (0.9.3) jammy; urgency=medium

  * fix(linux): snippet expansion (select text, press the expand-snippet
    shortcut) now works on GNOME Wayland even while you are still holding
    the shortcut's modifier keys. It now delivers the replacement through
    IBus as a direct D-Bus commit instead of a synthesized Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V
    keystroke, so it no longer depends on Mutter's per-device modifier
    state. Previously, if the expand-snippet hold lasted as long as a
    realistic press (Super+Shift held down), Mutter would merge those
    still-held keys into the synthesized Ctrl+C and the expansion silently
    failed every time. Verified 8/8 exact matches with modifiers held
    through the whole gesture, against a known-bad control on the old
    keystroke path that still failed 0/8 under the same conditions. Falls
    back to the previous copy/paste behavior when no receptive IBus target
    is found; picker auto-paste is unaffected.

 -- Aayush Gour <ag14906@gmail.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

clipnest (0.9.2) jammy; urgency=medium

  * fix(linux): the picker's Enter hint now says "Enter copy" instead of
    "Enter paste" whenever no auto-paste backend is available (e.g. a
    Wayland session without the uinput grant), and shows a one-time
    "Copied to clipboard — press Ctrl+V to paste. Auto-paste isn't set up
    on this session — see Settings -> Permissions" notice on the first
    paste attempt of the process. Previously this fallback was silent, so
    a user could not tell a working-as-designed clipboard-only session
    from a broken install.
  * fix(linux): AT-SPI snippet-expansion inserts are now verified by
    reading the affected text range back after a successful InsertText
    reply and comparing it to what was sent, instead of trusting the
    boolean reply alone. Mirrors the existing macOS AX trust contract; a
    mismatch is treated as a failed insert and falls back to the clipboard
    replacement path.

 -- Aayush Gour <ag14906@gmail.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

